NNAAMMEE
       pbmclean - despeckle a PBM image


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppbbmmcclleeaann [--mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss==_N] [--bbllaacckk|--wwhhiittee] [--eexxtteennddeedd] [_p_b_m_f_i_l_e]


OOPPTTIIOONN UUSSAAGGEE
       You  can  use  the minimum unique abbreviation of the options.  You can
       use two hyphens instead of one.  You can separate an option  name  from
       its value with white space instead of an equals sign.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmcclleeaann  cleans up a PBM image of random specks.  It reads a PBM image
       as input and outputs a PBM that is the same as the  input  except  with
       isolated pixels inverted.

       You can use ppbbmmcclleeaann  to clean up "snow" on bitmap images.

       There  are  two  ways ppbbmmcclleeaann can define "isolated" pixels: simple and
       extended.  When you specify --eexxtteennddeedd, ppbbmmcclleeaann uses  extended;  other‐
       wise it uses basic.


   BBaassiicc MMooddee
       In basic mode, ppbbmmcclleeaann looks at each pixel individually, and any pixel
       that doesn't have at least a minimum number of pixels of the same color
       touching it is considered isolated and ppbbmmcclleeaann erases it.

       The  --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss  option  specifies the minimum number of neighboring
       pixels of the same color for a pixel _n_o_t to be considered isolated.

       For example, if --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss is two and there are two contiguous black
       pixels  in  an otherwise white field, each of those pixels is isolated,
       so ppbbmmcclleeaann erases them - turns both white.

       The default minimum 1 pixel - ppbbmmcclleeaann flips only  completely  isolated
       pixels.

       (A  --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss  value greater than 8 generates a completely inverted
       image (but use ppnnmmiinnvveerrtt to do that) -- or a completely white  or  com‐
       pletely black image with the --bbllaacckk or --wwhhiittee option).

       ppbbmmcclleeaann  considers the area beyond the edges of the image to be white.
       (This matters when you consider pixels right on the edge of the image).

       ppbbmmcclleeaann does not distinguish between foreground and background; by de‐
       fault,  it  flips isolated pixels of either color.  But you can specify
       --bbllaacckk or --wwhhiittee to have it flip only pixels of one color.


   EExxtteennddeedd MMooddee
       In extended mode, ppbbmmcclleeaann erases all blobs which don't have the speci‐
       fied minimum number of pixels.  A blob is a set of contiguous pixels of
       the foreground color.  The minimum number of pixels  is  one  plus  the
       --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss  value.  You specify the foreground color with --bbllaacckk and
       --wwhhiittee (default is black).

       For example, if --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss is 2 and the foreground color  is  black,
       and  the  image contains a straight line 4 pixels long, ppbbmmcclleeaann erases
       that -- turns all four pixels white.  ppbbmmcclleeaann also erases 4 pixels  in
       a square or L-shape.

       The  default  --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss is 4, so a blob must have at least 5 pixels
       to escape ppbbmmcclleeaann's purge.

       Extended mode was new in Netpbm 10.56 (September 2011).



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppbbmmcclleeaann recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --bbllaacckk


       --wwhhiittee Flip pixels of the specified color.  By default, if you  specify
              neither  --bbllaacckk  nor --wwhhiittee, ppbbmmcclleeaann flips both black and white
              pixels which do not have sufficient identical neighbors.  If you
              specify  --bbllaacckk, ppbbmmcclleeaann leaves the white pixels alone and just
              erases isolated black pixels.  Vice versa for --wwhhiittee.   You  may
              specify  both  --bbllaacckk  and --wwhhiittee to get the same as the default
              behavior.


       --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss==_N
              This determines how many pixels must be in a  cluster  in  order
              for  ppbbmmcclleeaann to consider them legitimate and not clean them out
              of the image.  See Description ⟨#description⟩ .

              Before December 2001, ppbbmmcclleeaann accepted --_N instead of --mmiinnnneeiigghh‐‐
              bboorrss.  Before Netpbm 10.27 (March 2005), --mmiinnnneeiigghhbboorrss was --mmiinn‐‐
              nneeiigghhbboorr.


       --eexxtteennddeedd
              ppbbmmcclleeaann uses extended, as opposed to basic, isolated pixel  de‐
              tection.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.56 (September 2011).




